# Spec-Driven Development (SDD) Workflow

## Overview

This project strictly follows **Spec-Driven Development (TDD)** principles: every feature module begins with a detailed specification (spec.md, tasks.md, security.md) before any implementation code is written. The spec serves as the contract between design, security, and implementation.

---

## SDD Workflow for New Modules

### Recommended Path (Automated Spec Generation)

For most new modules:

```
1. Run /spec-draft "brief theme"
   ↓
   Agent proposes module slug, asks critical questions
   ↓
2. Respond to questions
   ↓
3. Review consolidated plan
   ↓
4. Confirm (or adjust, which loops back to Q&A)
   ↓
5. spec-architect generates:
   - specs/modules/NN-slug/spec.md (complete)
   - specs/modules/NN-slug/tasks.md (TDD checklist)
   - specs/modules/NN-slug/security.md (15 Laws)
   ↓
6. Run /spec-review NN-slug for final validation
   ↓
7. Run /module-start NN-slug to begin implementation
```

**Advantages**: Spec is rich with context; all 15 security laws pre-mapped; zero manual entry of boilerplate.

### Manual Path (Low-Level Scaffold)

Only when you have **all** details locked in and want direct control:

```
1. Run /spec-new <number> <slug>
   ↓
   Creates blank: specs/modules/NN-slug/{spec.md, tasks.md, security.md}
   ↓
2. Manually populate all three files
   ↓
3. Run /spec-review NN-slug
   ↓
4. Run /module-start NN-slug
```

**When to use**: Rare. Use /spec-draft first unless you have a very specific reason.

